Unix utility 'mkdir'

The Unix utility 'mkdir' creates a new, empty directory at a given path, absolute or
relative to the current directory, on the mounted volume. The new directory comes with its
own '.' and '..' entries already in place.

Every directory in the path given has to exist beforehand, since the utility creates only
the last name in it. A directory created by the root user comes out protected, in the same
way the 'protect' utility marks a path, so that only root can later remove or rename it.

USAGE:

mkdir <path>

Example of use:

mkdir /usr/local

It creates the directory local inside /usr, which has to exist already.

For more information, enter 'mkdir ?' or 'mkdir --help'.

HISTORY

